1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,039
Staging ferry checkout fails a payment
smoke test — or rather, succeeds against
the wrong place. The charge appears on the
production merchant dashboard.

2
00:00:09,159 --> 00:00:21,439
Root cause: application-prod.yml held
payment.merchant-id: ferry_live_9f3, and
the staging Deployment set
SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE empty.

3
00:00:21,559 --> 00:00:33,799
Only application.yml loaded, and someone
had "temporarily" put the live merchant id
in the default file during a firefight.
Boot profiles were supposed to keep
merchant IDs apart. Defaults betrayed
them.

4
00:00:33,919 --> 00:00:50,279
Boot builds on Framework profiles with
file conventions: application.yml plus
application-{profile}.yml, multi-document
YAML with spring.config.activate.on-
profile, and profile groups.

5
00:00:50,399 --> 00:01:04,039
Active profiles select which documents
join the Environment. The same artifact
carries every profile's config; activation
chooses the slice. Filename alone never
means "we are in prod." Look at this
example.

6
00:01:04,159 --> 00:01:16,199
# application.yml — safe defaults only
Look at this java for a moment. Walk the
documents. Default application.yml holds
only safe local values — never live
merchant IDs.

7
00:01:16,319 --> 00:01:28,719
The staging document activates only when
profile staging is on and overlays
payment.merchant-id. Prod document
likewise. PaymentProperties binds whatever
won in the Environment after activation.

8
00:01:28,839 --> 00:01:37,319
FerryPaymentClient.charge passes
props.merchantId() to the gateway — one
code path, environment-selected
credentials.

9
00:01:37,439 --> 00:01:51,279
Scrubbing defaults after the incident is
as important as setting
SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=staging on the
Deployment. Runtime with
SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=staging.

10
00:01:51,399 --> 00:02:02,919
Boot loads default documents, then staging
overlays payment.merchant-id →
ferry_staging_4a1. PaymentProperties binds
that value at context refresh.

11
00:02:03,039 --> 00:02:11,879
Empty active profiles leave
ferry_dev_local — or whatever unsafe value
you left in defaults, which is how live
ids leaked.

12
00:02:11,999 --> 00:02:18,959
Profile-specific beans (@Profile("prod")
payment circuit breakers) register only
when that profile is on.

13
00:02:19,079 --> 00:02:26,999
Groups like spring.profiles.group.producti
on=prod,metrics activate a bundle with one
name so ops sets a single token.

14
00:02:27,119 --> 00:02:40,559
Failure mode symptoms: staging smoke test
charge appears on prod merchant dashboard;
payment.merchant-id in a secured env dump
shows ferry_live_9f3 while hostname is
staging;

15
00:02:40,679 --> 00:02:44,719
Environment.getActiveProfiles() empty or
unexpectedly prod.

16
00:02:44,839 --> 00:02:55,799
Another failure: both staging and prod
active by misconfiguration — last-wins
overlay rules and document order decide
the merchant id; do not rely on "both" as
a feature for payments.

17
00:02:55,919 --> 00:03:03,079
CI that never asserts active profiles on a
staging deploy will miss this class of bug
until money moves. Trade-offs.

18
00:03:03,199 --> 00:03:12,119
Boot profile documents keep one JAR for
all environments and pair cleanly with
@ConfigurationProperties; they concentrate
risk in activation and default hygiene.

19
00:03:12,239 --> 00:03:16,879
Separate artifacts per environment avoid
activation mistakes and multiply build
pipelines.

20
00:03:16,999 --> 00:03:29,239
Prefer safe defaults + mandatory profile
in non-local envs (fail startup if
prod/staging missing when a property
app.require-profile=true) over clever
multi-profile bags for payment config.

21
00:03:29,359 --> 00:03:42,639
Compare Framework @Profile on beans with
Boot's document overlays: both honor
spring.profiles.active, but Boot adds the
file/document convention so merchant IDs
can live in YAML without alternate @Bean
methods.

22
00:03:42,759 --> 00:03:57,039
The ferry bug was a document problem —
wrong values in the default file — not a
missing @Profile on a class. When both
exist, activation still gates everything;
Boot does not invent a profile from a
hostname.

23
00:03:57,159 --> 00:04:09,119
Put live secrets and merchant IDs only in
profile-specific documents or external
mounts, and keep defaults harmless enough
that an empty SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE
cannot charge real cards.

24
00:04:09,239 --> 00:04:18,120
A staging smoke test that asserts
payment.merchant-id starts with
ferry_staging_ catches this class of bug
before money moves.

25
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:28,360
Misconception unique to Boot profiles:
"application-prod.yml is used
automatically in production because the
filename contains prod." Filename alone
does nothing.

26
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:41,000
Something must activate prod — env var,
config server, or argument. Filename is a
convention for which document binds when
that profile is active. Merchant IDs stay
in the right environments after defaults
are scrubbed.

27
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:54,759
Platform still ships a 180MB fat jar into
every tram-timetable container layer, and
image pulls dominate deploy time. How Boot
packages that jar — fat versus layered —
is the remaining deploy-shape problem.
